"**Answer**\n\nThe only works in the U.S. GSA Fine Arts Collection that are *not* lithographic prints (i.e., that have another print technique such as woodcut or crayon) are:\n\n| Rank | Title (if listed) | Date | Medium | GSA location on the collection page |\n|------|-------------------|------|--------|-------------------------------------|\n| 1 | *Countryside* | 1939 | Color\u202fwoodcut on paper | **Los\u202fAngeles\u202fCounty\u202fMuseum\u202fof\u202fArt (LACMA)** |\n| 2 | *Untitled* (woodcut) | 1937 | Woodcut printed in color on Japan paper | **University of\u2011California, Los\u202fAngeles (UCLA)** |\n| 3 | *Untitled* (crayon) | 1937 | Color crayon & brush and tusche painting | **San\u202fDiego Museum of Art (SDMOA)** |\n| 4 | *Untitled* (other color\u2011crayon) | 1939 | Color crayon with tusche | **Museum of the American West** |\n\nAmong these, the **youngest**\u2014the most recent in date\u2014is the 1939 **woodcut \u201cCountryside.\u201d**  \nThe U.S. GSA records state that this work is on loan to the **Los\u202fAngeles\u202fCounty\u202fMuseum\u202fof\u202fArt (LACMA)**, which is located at 5905\u202fS. Dale\u202fDr., Los\u202fAngeles, CA.\n\n**Thus, the westernmost museum that holds this youngest non\u2011lithographic work is the Los\u202fAngeles\u202fCounty\u202fMuseum\u202fof\u202fArt (LACMA).**"